WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.920
What's up, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners,

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:06.340
episode 38 for May 14th, 2026.

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:09.380
Let's break down today's coolest AI news

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:10.840
so anyone can understand it.

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:11.880
Let's go.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.940
So imagine you are juggling 10 different browser tabs

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:18.780
while working on a school project about electric cars.

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:21.780
One tab has a review of battery life,

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:24.200
another shows prices for different models,

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:27.860
and a third has a long article on charging stations.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:32.660
Microsoft just upgraded the AI helper inside its edge browser

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:34.820
so it can see and understand everything

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:37.580
across all of those open tabs at the same time.

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:41.080
Instead of asking the chatbot about one page at a time,

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.800
you can now ask it to compare two products

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.480
you're looking at in different tabs.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.700
Pull facts from multiple articles

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.880
or even turn your research into a quick podcast summary.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:53.980
Think of it like having a super organized friend

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:56.600
who can glance at every open window on your screen

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.920
and instantly connect the dots between them.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:01.800
The update also adds a memory feature

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:04.380
that remembers details from earlier conversations

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:07.580
and a quiz mode that can test you on what you just read.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:10.980
For students or anyone doing school projects,

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:14.340
this means less time copying and pasting between tabs

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.520
and more time actually understanding the material.

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:20.160
It could change how people research online

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:23.120
by making the browser itself part of the thinking process

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:25.320
rather than just a window to information.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:29.100
If you're already using edge on your laptop or phone,

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:31.080
this feature is rolling out now

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:34.220
and works with the free version of the company's AI tool

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:35.260
called co-pilot.

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:40.000
To try it, open edge, start a chat with co-pilot in the sidebar

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.920
and ask something like compare the phones in my other tabs

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.940
while you have a few product pages open.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:49.020
The more tabs you have, the more useful it gets

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.220
for real tasks like planning a purchase

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:53.540
or studying a topic from several sources.

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:56.540
Okay, now from my favorite part of the show,

00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:59.240
the deep dive where we actually look under the hood.

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:02.700
Today's deep dive is all about why AI writing

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:04.600
sometimes feels strangely formal

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:07.440
with those long dashes and perfect sentences

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:09.380
that nobody uses in real texts.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:11.780
You know how when you're texting a friend,

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:13.880
your phone's keyboard guesses the next word

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:15.360
before you even finish typing?

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.380
Now imagine that same autocomplete

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:20.760
got way better at noticing tiny patterns

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:23.260
across millions of old messages and books.

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:26.060
First it learns that certain punctuation marks

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:29.100
like the long dash often appear in polished writing,

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:32.340
even if most people rarely use them in casual chats.

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:35.040
Then it starts copying that style

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:36.800
because the pattern shows up a lot

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:39.760
and the training data from professional articles and essays.

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:42.760
Next, when you ask it to write something,

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:45.080
it reaches for those same polished patterns

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:47.360
because they feel like the smart way to sound

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:50.340
and that's basically what a language model is doing

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:53.160
when it fills its answers with M dashes,

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:55.160
even though regular internet comments

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:57.240
are full of typos and short sentences.

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:01.340
So next time someone says AI writing looks weirdly formal,

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:04.400
you can tell them it's just autocomplete on steroids

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:07.060
that learn from the fanciest examples it could find.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.660
Not so mysterious once you see it that way.

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:12.940
Let's move on to some cool stuff you can try right now.

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:17.700
Meta just added an incognito chat mode to its AI on WhatsApp

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.800
and the Meta AI app where conversations get processed

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.380
without being saved anywhere,

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:24.520
even on Meta's own servers.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:27.020
This is exciting because it gives you a way

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:28.540
to ask questions or brainstorm

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:31.460
without worrying that your words stick around forever.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:35.020
Anyone who uses WhatsApp already can try it right away

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:37.300
by starting a chat with Meta AI

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:39.140
and switching to the private option.

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:43.520
Go into WhatsApp, open a conversation with the Meta AI bot

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:46.440
and type something like, help me plan a weekend trip,

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:49.920
then look for the incognito toggle to keep it from saving.

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:53.080
It's a simple way to test private AI use

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:54.940
without signing up for anything new.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:58.700
Next up is an app called Poppy that connects your calendar,

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:02.260
email and messages to notice what you need to do next

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:04.160
and surface reminders automatically.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:06.800
It feels like having a helpful assistant

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:09.440
that knows your schedule without you typing everything in.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:13.280
Students or anyone juggling school and hobby should try it

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:15.460
because it can pull tasks from real messages

00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:17.380
instead of making you copy them over.

00:04:17.960 --> 00:04:20.500
Head to the Poppy website or App Store listing,

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:22.760
connect your email and calendar accounts,

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:24.600
then ask it to show reminders

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:26.280
based on what's coming up this week.

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:30.020
You may need a parent's help to sign up if you're under 18,

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:33.020
but the setup is straightforward and free to start.

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:36.260
Time for quick bits, those fast little updates

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:37.540
that caught my eye today.

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:41.460
Amazon is putting its Alexa Plus AI directly

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:43.940
into the search bar on amazon.com.

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:47.140
So typing a question like best headphones for school

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:49.860
now gets answers from the AI shopping helper

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:51.460
instead of just product lists.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:54.820
It's interesting because it turns everyday shopping

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:57.920
into a conversation where the AI can suggest options

00:04:57.920 --> 00:04:59.740
based on what you actually need.

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:02.940
Rivian just added a new voice assistant

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:05.560
to its electric vehicles that can control features

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:07.960
and answer questions without needing your phone.

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:11.540
This stands out because it shows AI assistance moving

00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:14.120
from apps into the things you use every day,

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:15.400
like your car dashboard.

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:16.820
And that's a wrap.

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:19.060
If any of today's stories made you go,

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:20.120
huh, that's cool.

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:21.580
Go play with it.

00:05:21.940 --> 00:05:24.180
Curiosity is how every expert started.

